{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"[operator-attested] evidence for the two criteria whose proof is a TEST RUN and therefore lives outside the repository.\n\nCHANGE LANDED. Gerrit change 1669 MERGED as 61058f6085 (local be915ab89c, rebased server-side by Rebase-If-Necessary), with LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1. The Verified +1 IS the full-suite pass: CI run https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/31630232865 went green on every job, including pytest on ubuntu-latest py3.11 (13m10s), py3.12 (13m56s), py3.13 (13m20s) and macos-latest py3.13, plus lint + type-check, mutation shards, artifact provenance, optionality, golden-path and verify-identity. macOS matters specifically here: it is the no-/proc platform this bug is about, so that leg exercises the changed branch on the platform where _process_start_time returns None.\n\nCRITERION 1 — the split is behaviour-preserving. Commands run in the worktree with its venv on PATH (env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\"):\n\n  python -m pytest tests/unit/test_lock_age_ceiling.py tests/unit/test_lock_container_recreate.py tests/unit/test_lock_stale_reclaim.py tests/unit/test_lock_timeout_names_holder_7084.py tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py tests/unit/test_write_lock_busy_probe_7084.py -q\n  -> 76 passed in 31.29s\n\n  python -m pytest tests/unit -q -k 'lock or store or concur or txn or append'\n  -> 765 passed, 4 skipped in 172.96s\n\n  make lint  -> All checks passed (ruff check + format-check + complexity baseline + zizmor + actionlint)\n  make typecheck -> Success: no issues found in 510 source files\n\nBoth reference forms were repointed and neither was shimmed. A first cut of the split used 'from lock_owner import <name>' inside lock.py, which created a SECOND binding; monkeypatch on lock_owner then did not reach describe_lock_holder and test_describe_never_raises_on_an_unreadable_stamp FAILED. That failure is the direct evidence that the repoint is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. Fixed by reaching every moved name module-qualified, leaving exactly one binding, and pinned going forward by test_lock_module_does_not_re_export_the_moved_private_names, which asserts the moved names are absent from lock's namespace and present in lock_owner's.\n\nCRITERION 2 — the reported defect no longer reproduces. The reproduction is test_recycled_pid_with_no_proc_is_reclaimed_only_past_the_ceiling in tests/unit/test_lock_age_ceiling.py: it stamps a lock with a pid that IS alive but is NOT the process that wrote the stamp (a reaped pid via _dead_pid(), with _pid_alive forced True and _process_start_time forced None to model a platform with no /proc), which is exactly the reported scenario. Under the OLD code _mkdir_lock_is_stale returned False for that stamp at ANY age, which is the unbounded wedge. It now asserts False while fresh and True once aged past _MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S, so the wedge is bounded and the lock is not broken early.\n\nThe pre-existing test that PINNED the old behaviour, test_aged_live_pid_with_unknown_start_is_NOT_reclaimed_by_the_ceiling, described itself as 'THE BOUNDARY ... research rec #6, deliberately NOT this change'. That is precisely the gap this bug closes, so it was deliberately inverted, with its docstring and the module docstring rewritten to say why. The inversion of a test that previously encoded the defect is itself end-to-end evidence the behaviour changed.\n\nNot-broken-early is covered by test_aged_live_owner_is_never_reclaimed (corroborated start times, still refused far past the ceiling) and test_recycled_pid_with_a_known_differing_start_is_reclaimed_at_once (proven recycled, reclaimed on evidence rather than a timer)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786565222780694001,"uuid":"0d8cda6b-310e-4656-9d71-920283b34a66"}